From: FineWine
Subject: [PATCH] ntdll: Search WINEDLLPATH before Wine's own DLL directory.

Upstream looks in its own DLL directory first and in WINEDLLPATH afterwards, so a DLL there can
add to Wine's built-in set but never replace a member of it.

FineWine ships graphics layers beside the runtime (DXMT's d3d11.dll, dxgi.dll and d3d10core.dll
under lib/dxmt) and selects one per prefix by putting its folder in WINEDLLPATH. For that to
take effect the folder has to win over the built-in DLL of the same name, so the order is
reversed. With WINEDLLPATH unset nothing changes.
---
diff --git a/dlls/ntdll/unix/loader.c b/dlls/ntdll/unix/loader.c
index 42b6987..9dfaa6b 100644
--- a/dlls/ntdll/unix/loader.c
+++ b/dlls/ntdll/unix/loader.c
@@ -303,8 +303,9 @@ static void set_dll_path(void)
     dll_paths = malloc( (count + 2) * sizeof(*dll_paths) );
     count = 0;
 
-    if (!build_dir) dll_paths[count++] = dll_dir;
-
+    /* FineWine: directories in WINEDLLPATH are searched before Wine's own, so that a graphics
+     * layer shipped beside the runtime (DXMT's d3d11.dll, for one) replaces the built-in DLL of
+     * the same name when a prefix selects it. Upstream searches Wine's own directory first. */
     if (path)
     {
         path = strdup(path);
@@ -312,6 +313,8 @@ static void set_dll_path(void)
         free( path );
     }
 
+    if (!build_dir) dll_paths[count++] = dll_dir;
+
     for (i = 0; i < count; i++) dll_path_maxlen = max( dll_path_maxlen, strlen(dll_paths[i]) );
     dll_paths[count] = NULL;
 }
